 identifying a  disease that you might be interested in  attempting to treat and getting to the  clinical phase of actually testing your  treatment in people  so we wanted to use our skill set at  engineering cell lines to try to  both improve the cost and shorten the  timeline for our customers  and when we look at this timeline once a  disease has been selected to potentially  target  a target id needs to be identified and  or a pathway  many assays need to be developed in  order to test these  targets or pathways in vitro and most  often or not these include some sort of  high throughput screening assay  and then once the customer has a hit  that they are interested in pursuing  there is also a space for us within the  um  lot release and pre-clinical testing of  whatever  pharmaceutical or  or target that they are interested in  identifying  so where we thought that we could fit in  is within the assay development  phase of this where we can engineer cell  lines that our customers can use  to screen for their targets or for any  sort of lot release essay  and when we think about what these  targets could potentially be  this is a screenshot from a figure from  a paper published in 2019  that shows the target landscape compared  from drugs in the pipeline in 2017 to  2019 so only two years difference  what we can see here is obviously there  is a  wide increase in the number of targets  that are going to be investigated within  just those two years  in addition to that  the size of each of these bubbles is  directly related to how many potential  drugs are targeting each of these  targets  it boxed in red on the right are three  such targets including cd19 egfr and ny  eso one which are three of the targets  that we decided to pursue for some of  our off-the-shelf cell lines  that could potentially be used for early  drug discovery as well as pre-clinical  testing  so while working with our customers to  engineer their cell lines we have  identified some challenges within the  field  these start with the development  pipeline including target identification  from patient samples so once a disease  has been identified as a customer being  interested in potentially targeting  these patient samples prove very  valuable to try to identify either  mutated genes or pathways that could  potentially be a target  however the utilization of transformed  cell lines for clinical pre-discovery  and compound reagent testing are also  equally important since patient samples  do not grow typically grow very well in  culture  so the current limitations within this  pipeline  include things like in vitro testing  that requires negative control cells  that do not express the target of  interest and positive control cells  which do express the target of interest  and what we have found is that currently  used control cells may not be from the  same person organ or even lineage which  can lead to high background and false  positive and negative results  so we have identified that the field  needs non-passage limited easy-to-grow  and scalable cell lines that are  pertinent to clinical endpoints  if these cells are amenable to 96 384  well assays have affordable and easy  culture conditions and are able to  produce robust and reproducible results  and this is where we thought that our  strengths in cell line engineering could  come into play  this is an example of some of the target  cell lines that are typically used in  drug discovery and preclinical testing  this is a paper from 2013 which was  looking at two b-cell lymphoma targets  cd19 and cd22  positive and negative controls were  required for these assays so a cell line  that expresses  cd19 but not cd22 or cd22 and not cd19  and what this group did was they simply  took the available cell lines that are  easy to culture  and they assayed them for how much  protein they expressed on the surface as  shown in this panel on the upper  left-hand side  and what you can see on the bottom part  b  is that these cell lines do express  varying levels of each of these proteins  but when we look at the primary patient  samples on the right hand side of the  slide they also express varying levels  of these proteins  you can easily see that this is a very  complicated set of cells to attempt to  use for any sort of pre-clinical testing  or lot release  since  they express varying different varying  different levels of the protein  so we asked the question of what if  instead of using a mixture of cell types  from different organs or tissues or  lineages you could use a matched panel  of cells that grew with the same media  and similar growth kinetics and  morphology for your pre-clinical studies  and we set to do this  for what we're calling our  tumor-associated antigen panel cell  lines  we have identified parental cancer cell  lines that grow very well in culture and  typically express the tumor-associated  energy of interest endogenously  we perform genetic knockout experiments  using zinc finger nucleases to render  these cells null  for this tumor-associated antigen of  interest thus giving you a negative  control cell line  we then took these knockout cells  and we delivered the tumor-associated  antigen of interest via lentivirus  and we perform single cell cloning and  identified clones that express  differential levels of the proteins  thus giving you one cell line that  expresses low levels of the protein and  another cell line that expresses high  levels of the protein  but all of these came from the parental  cell line they grow the same in culture  use the same media conditions and they  are amenable to high throughput screens  the three cell lines that we chose to  use for our three tumor-associated  antigen panels that we'll be presenting  today  include ragi cells which are a b-cell  lymphoma cell line  that express the cd19 antigen of  interest  a549 cells which are lung cancer cell  lines which express egfr and a375 cells  which express the nyeso1 type  this is the data that we generated on  our rajis19 antigen panel  cd19 is a cell surface molecule that is  expressed on the surface of b-cells and  highly expressed on the surface of  b-cell lymphomas  we generated our knockouts using zfns  and used next generation sequencing to  confirm the genotype of these cells as  well as extensive phenotyping using flow  cytometry  on the right side of this slide you can  see that our knockout cell line does not  express cd18 on the surface  and we were able to find two single cell  clones that expressed either a low level  of cd19 shown in red or a high level of  cd19 shown in blue and i would like to  point out that these low and high  phrases that i am using are in general  relative to the endogenous level of the  protein on the provincial fill line  before we engineer the knockout  for our egfr panel we chose a549 lung  cancer cells  egfr is a receptor tyrosine kinase that  is known to be mutated in many different  types of cancer including lung cancers  we again used zfns to generate knockouts  in a549 cells there are actually three  copies of egfr within the genome so we  were able to knock out all three copies  within our parental knockout cell line  and then when we redelivered the egfr  expression using lentivirus we were  again shown on the right able to find a  low expressor and a high expressor  in addition to delivering wildpipe egfr  into this panel  we also delivered a commonly found  mutant of egfr so egfr v3  is a  naturally occurring variant of egfr  often found in cancers  this  is an in-frame deletion which actually  causes egfr to lose its ligand  dependence and become constitutively  active so it is always signaling when  you have this egfr v3 mutation we took  the same egfr parental knockout cell  line that i showed you on the previous  slide  and this time we delivered the egfr v3  variant via lentivirus  and what was interesting is that we were  unable to find a low or a high  expressing clone all of the clones that  we acted had the same level of egfr on  the surface  and when we think about this  biologically it makes a lot of sense  because this is a receptor tyrosine  kinase that performs intracellular  signaling upon binding with a ligand but  since this variant 3  loses its ligand dependence it's always  on so you can think of it as  egfr typically works as a dimmer switch  so either it's off in the absence of  ligand if you have a little bit of  ligand it might be a dim signaling  pathway but if you have a lot of ligand  it's going to be signaling quite a bit  with the v3 it's more like an on off  either you have the v3 and you have  signaling or you don't express v3 and  you don't see any signaling and that  data is shown on the right hand side of  the slide where we were only able to  find one level of egfr v3 expression  however when we used a pan-egfr antibody  we do see quite high expression of this  protein on the cell surface  the third tumor-associated antigen panel  that i would like to talk about today is  a little bit different  so ny eso 1 is not actually a protein  coding gene it is a peptide so it is  encoded by two genes c tag one and c tag  two which are present on the x  chromosome  the nye so 1 is a derivative of the  protein product of these two genes but  it is a small peptide that has been  found to be presented on the surface of  many different cancer cells  and this includes colon cancer and a375  cells are a commonly used colon cancer  derived cell line  so this was a little more complicated  since um  the nye so peptide is potentially  encoded by two different genes but again  using our zsn system we were able to  cleanly find clones that had a knockout  of all of the c tags one and two genes  shown in the middle panel using our next  generation sequencing pathway  in addition the ny etho is not as i said  it's not a full protein it's more like a  peptide  so instead of using a flow cytometry  phenotyping assay we decided to use a  western blotting assay so that we could  see all of the peptide that was  potentially expressed within the cell  and you can clearly see that our  knockout cell line in the first lane  does not express any ctag gene product  and we in this case we were able to find  both low expressing clones and high  expressing clones  so to summarize i have shown you data on  our anagen panels that include cancer  relevant cell pipes that express varying  levels of the antigen of interest  and we feel that these tools will be  useful for early drug discovery antibody  compound testing or assay development  and due to our experience in custom cell  line engineering if this is something  that is of interest of you to you we can  also develop custom antigen panels in  your cell line of choice so whether you  have a particular cell line that you  work in or a target of interest that we  have not covered in today's talk please  reach out to us and we can  generate a custom cell line for your  uses  so to change gears a little bit um the  next panel that i would like to talk  about is our hla panel so so far i've  presented data on tumor-associated  antigen expressing cells but something  that we need to remember within the  immuno ecology field is that it's not as  simple as a cell line just expressing a  target of interest  so  when we think about the immune system  targeting cancer cell lines or tumor  cells  we need to also think about specifically  the t cells so i really like this  cartoon on the left hand side of this  slide where you have a somatic cell  that's expressing a protein or a peptide  of interest within an mhc class one and  a cytotoxic t cell comes along and says  i'm sorry but i don't recognize the mhc  peptide complex you have there  and it makes him sad because now the t  cell does not recognize him can't kill  him can't do anything and just goes  along his way  so what we need to remember here is that  t cells will only be stimulated to  target tumor cells when the tcr  recognizes an mhc bound peptide  and these hla dependent therapies can  include cytotoxic t lymphocytes as well  as peptide rna or a dna vaccine  and to look at this a little bit more  in detail this is a cartoon that  i'm using from a paper published in 2008  which shows on the bottom an  antigen-presenting cell and on the top  um an mhc  hla b8 restricted t cell  so the t cells will only recognize  peptides as presented by hla b8 on the  left hand side of this slide this is a t  cell that can recognize this peptide in  the hla b8 groove  however in the middle  we have the t cell and we have the  correct peptide but the antigen  presenting cell instead of putting it in  hla b8 it is putting it in hla b44 so  even though it's exactly the same  peptide the t cell does not recognize it  because it doesn't see that mhc type 1.  on the right hand side the antigen  presenting cell is expressing the  correct hla but it is actually  presenting a different peptide so that  also is not recognized by the t cell so  what we can think of with this system is  a very discreet  lock and key mechanism  where the t cell you can think of as  lock in the antigen presenting cell as  the key and everything needs to  completely match up for that t cell to  recognize the antigen presenting cell  and potentially kill it which is what  we're looking for with immunotherapy  so again using our expertise in gene  engineering we thought well this makes  for a very  complicated potential in vitro testing  system  um  when you think about hla class mhc class  ones  you receive hlas a b and c  one copy from each of your parents  though so that's potentially six  different hlas that you're presenting on  the surface  and while that's really great for the  biology of your immune system because it  gives your t cells a lot of diversity  it can make assays with these cells  quite difficult and the data can be a  little bit messy  so using a protocol that was first  published in 2015 we thought okay well  what if we can knock out all of the  endogenous mhc class ones on our cell  lines and only produce one um and that  way you're not limited by the cancer  cell line anymore if the cancer cell if  the cancer cell line of interest that  you're using only expresses hla a1  but the t cell that you're interested in  testing expresses or recognizes hla a2  you can no longer use that cancer cell  line but what if we can take that cell  line that you're used to culturing and  you're very comfortable with and we can  just engineer it to express hla a2 then  you have a system that you can use in  your lab  so  instead of the laborious task of  attempting to knock out six different  genes all of which differ by only a  grouping of single nucleotide  polymorphisms  we decided to knock out a partner  protein known as beta2 microglobulin  this is shown in the diagram on the  lower left hand side  beta 2m is an important part of mhc  class 1 because it is what chaperones  the mhc complex mhc class 1 complex to  the cell surface so without beta 2m even  though you still produce hla protein it  cannot get to the surface and when we  think about t cells interacting with  tumor cells if it's not on the surface  the t cell can't see it  so we use the fm to knock out the beta  2m gene  and we carefully confirmed that this  were true knockouts so we did this using  sequencing as well as phenotyping assays  just to make sure that we weren't having  breakthrough or erroneous expression of  the hlas on the surface and we have not  seen that in any of the cells that  we've tested  so once we have this beta 2m null cell  line we again use lentivirus to deliver  single hlas into the cell  and we confirmed the expression via  phenotyping  so we chose three different cell lines  we used a549 cells again our lung cancer  cell line the jerkit cell line which is  a t lymphocyte cell line and raji cells  which are a b lymphocyte cell line  and on the right hand side of the slide  we picked some of the most commonly  found  human mhc class 1 shown on the right we  focused on hla a and hla b for this  first panel  and we expressed each of these hla  singularly within the cells  in this slide is a representation of  some of the data that we generated using  these panels so for this  example i am showing data from the a549  hla panel  on the upper right hand corner  wild type a549 cells are shown here in  blue expressing beta 2m on the surface  and our knockout cell lines are shown in  red so when we knock out beta 2m you no  longer get beta2m on the surface we have  also stained these cells with pan  mhc class 1 antibodies and similar to  the beta 2m expression we also do not  see any expression of hlas on the  surface in our knockout cell line  once we delivered each of the single  shown here into this knockout parental  phone line  you can see very clearly that we get  high expression of beta 2m on the  surface and again when we stain these  cells with a pan mhc class 1 antibody  we see similar results so we are getting  high expression of not only the beta 2m  but also the hla on the surface and we  have also done internal testing  looking for  whether any of the endogenous hlas are  pulled to the surface once we over  express these hlas  however with our lentiviral construction  we have fused beta-2m to each of these  hla types so we only see the fusion  protein product on the surface we don't  see any of the endogenous hlas but we  have carefully looked for each of these  panels  so to summarize the second part of the  talk we have developed cancer relevant  cell types that express individual mhc  class 1 alleles and we have done this in  8549 cells joicit cells and rawgi cells  and i only showed data for the a549s but  the data for the jirkets and the rajes  look very similar  these cell-based assays can be used to  evaluate tcr therapies as well as  peptide and oncolytic vaccine  development um and just to point out if  you have other hlas that you are  interested in expression expressing or  another cell line um other than the  three that i've talked about today we  can of course do this on a custom basis  so i hope that i've convinced you that  the utilization of one cell line that  expresses varying levels of a  tumor-associated antigen will aid in  your pre-clinical discovery and  validation  and that monoxilic hla class 1  expressing cells will be a useful tool  for any immuno oncologist interested in  peptide presentation and t-cell killing  assays  the data that i've shown today is all  encompassed within our off-the-shelf  tumor-associated antigen and hla cell  lines  you can find the website here  in addition as i mentioned we do provide  customs online engineering as well so  please visit our cell design studio  webpage and  we would be happy to provide you with  any additional information you may want  so just to finish up  using our expertise in tailoring  cellular models to customer needs we can  perform any sort of modification that  you might envision including knockouts  targeted integration we can deliver  clones or pools  we are very experienced in induced  pluripotent stem cells and primary stem  cell culture  and any of these cell lines can be used  for any number of drug discovery  applications or other applications even  if you're not within the drug discovery  field and we really do look at ourselves  as research partners  we are not a cro we are a partner with  you so you will have access to our phd  level scientists and over a decade of  experience in engineering cells and  it's rare that we find a cell type that  we have not engineered in the past so i  encourage you to please reach out to us  with any questions you may have  so with that i would like to thank you  for your attention and i hope that  you have a wonderful day thanks

